/-!
# The original Mathieu application of the cubic six-cover comparison

Keep every argument of the marked Mathieu recognition adapter and use its
same IsTypeJ4 witness. Given the explicit earlier section 4 local data and
original-H perfectness on both sides, construct an isomorphism of the actual
cubic centralizers in the source and native J₄. It commutes with the literal
M₂₂ projections and preserves both the cubic scalar and marked involution.
The same original splitting element gives the normalizer factorization.
The comparison now also intertwines all normalizer actors and the actual
quadratic core modules through consistent triple-cover coordinates. The
quadratic comparison lifts to the actual extraspecial cores, intertwines the
whole six-cover, and glues to O²(H) with both restriction equations. Full
normalizer equivariance constructs the plane central-quotient comparison.
The matching splitting actor now has square one, and extension over its
actual cyclic subgroup gives the full plane isomorphism, retaining its
core, scalar cubic, six-cover restriction and full normalizer. Both
source outer automorphisms are constructed on the original local groups.
The point-group application constructs its Todd comparison, retaining the
M₂₄ quotient conjugator for both the trio and sextet, the marker, and the
literal point-plane, point-line and triple overlaps.

These are the local steps of AS91 section 8, pp.605–607, applied to the original
Aschbacher–Smith 8.2.12–14 inputs, pp.722–723. The plane flag comparison aligns both
plane overlaps and all local edges inside H. The overlap kernel on Q/Z(Q)
is now generated by actual inner corrections through Q and the plane twist.
The general overlap action is classified. The source Input now supplies
its actual edge to the proved cubic-normalizer formula and the constructed
outer Todd correction preserving the entire edge fixer. The Recognition export
now completes edge alignment and full reversal, constructs the source-base
isomorphism and applies it to the original Mathieu branch. Its explicit native
premises are LocalData, original-H cubic-normalizer data and the actual local
generation equality. Both local restrictions and the original marker are retained;
unconditional native construction and final exclusion remain with their owners.
-/

